---
title: Performance
description: Where the time and allocation savings come from, the benchmark numbers, and how to read them honestly.
---

The design premise is that all dispatch-shape work happens at compile time or once per
message type, never per dispatch. This page shows what that buys and what it does not.

## Where the work goes

**Compile time.** The source generator pre-computes handler descriptors and emits dispatch
roots that close the runtime's generic executor and invoker types over each message. Nothing
has to be discovered or constructed at startup.

**Once per `(message type, group set)`.** The pipeline plan is built and cached
process-wide: six fixed stages, each a pre-ordered array, with covariant matching resolved
and generic handler types already closed. Group filters and `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` are
applied here, once.

**Every dispatch.** Rent a pooled context, look up the cached executor for the message's
runtime type, walk the plan, resolve handler *instances* from the calling scope, return the
context. No reflection, no `MakeGenericType`, no registry scan, no `AsyncLocal`.

The executor holds its own resolved plan, re-validated against the registry version on each
dispatch — a field read and an integer compare. A runtime registration bumps the version and
the next dispatch rebuilds. Group-less dispatch, which is nearly all of it, is keyed by
message type alone, so no group set is materialized and no composite key is hashed.

## Pooled contexts

Execution contexts are rented from a `[ThreadStatic]`-first pool — a plain load and store on
the common same-thread cycle — and returned on completion. A synchronous fast path skips the
async state machine entirely when the whole pipeline completes synchronously.

This is the single largest contributor to the allocation numbers below, and it is the source
of the one rule the API imposes: a context is valid only for the duration of its dispatch.
See [Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).

## Typed all the way down

Executors and invokers are closed over each message's concrete runtime type, so handlers are
invoked through their typed members with no object-typed bridge anywhere. `MakeGenericType`
survives only as a fallback for open generics and runtime-only registrations.

## The straight-through path

The common pipeline is not a pipeline at all: one handler, no interceptors, no result
adapters. The executor recognises that shape when it builds its plan, and dispatch then calls
the handler's typed member and returns its `ValueTask` unchanged — no strategy object, no
async state machine, no interceptor-count checks. Add a single interceptor and the message
goes back through the full staged pipeline; the fast path is an optimisation of the empty
case, not a different execution model.

Both this and the executor's cached plan live in the pipeline executors, which means they
cover `SendAsync`, `QueryAsync` and `IMessageMediator.DispatchAsync`. **Event publishing and
streaming queries do not go through them.** `PublishAsync` broadcasts to every matching
handler by design, and `StreamAsync` returns an enumerable the caller drains after the call
returns; both dispatch through the options path, resolving dependencies per dispatch and
building an unpooled context. The numbers on this page do not describe those two paths.

## Mediator lifetime

The mediator facades — `ICommandMediator`, `IQueryMediator`, `IEventMediator`, `IPublisher`,
`IMessageMediator` — are registered **transient**. They are stateless; the only thing they
capture is the provider that resolved them, and a transient service receives the resolving
scope's provider exactly as a scoped one does. Handlers still resolve from the calling scope.

The difference is what registration costs. Resolving a scoped service takes the scope's lock
and writes the instance into the scope's resolved-services dictionary — a cost that amortizes
across a long-lived scope, and never amortizes at all when every dispatch gets its own scope.
That is the web-server row in the table below, and it is why these facades are transient.

## Benchmarks

BenchmarkDotNet with `[MemoryDiagnoser]`. Each operation performs **100 000 sequential
dispatches** of a no-op handler.

Environment: BenchmarkDotNet v0.15.8, Windows 11, AMD Ryzen 7 7800X3D, .NET 9.0.11
(RyuJIT x86-64-v4). Measured on the `preview` line, 2026-07-28.

Two shapes are measured, for three mediators:

- **Typical usage** — how an application normally sends messages: the library's public
  mediator interface, one shared DI scope for the whole loop.
- **Web-server shape** — a fresh DI scope for every dispatch, mimicking one scope per HTTP
  request, which is the shape ASP.NET Core gives you.

| Scenario (100k dispatches/op) | Mean | Allocated | Gen0/1k ops |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| **Ergosfare** — typical usage | **2.97 ms** | **2.29 MB** | 47 |
| **MediatR** — typical usage | 5.79 ms | 18.31 MB | 375 |
| **LiteBus** — typical usage | 139.62 ms | 714.87 MB | 14 750 |
| **Ergosfare** — web-server shape | **8.46 ms** | **21.36 MB** | 438 |
| **MediatR** — web-server shape | 10.28 ms | 33.57 MB | 688 |
| Ergosfare — internal engine path (reference row) | 6.31 ms | 5.34 MB | 109 |

Reproduce with:

```bash
dotnet run -c Release -f net9.0 --project test/Stella.Ergosfare.Benchmarking
```

## Reading the table honestly

**The typical-usage rows are the headline.** Ergosfare runs the loop in about half MediatR's
time while allocating about an eighth of its garbage, with Gen0 collection pressure down
accordingly. The allocation gap comes from pooled contexts and the `ValueTask`-first pipeline;
the time gap comes from the straight-through path and the executor's cached plan.

**The web-server shape is the closer race.** Ergosfare is ahead on both axes here — but by
roughly 18% on time, against roughly 36% on allocation. Read the time margin as *ahead*, not
as *decisively ahead*: this row is dominated by DI scope creation, which both libraries pay
identically, and repeated runs on this machine put MediatR's row anywhere between 10.3 and
11.5 ms. The allocation margin is the stable one. If your workload is one dispatch per HTTP
request, this row describes you — and next to the cost of the request itself, neither margin
is decisive.

This row is also the one that moved most in this release: it read 18.81 ms / 38.91 MB in
v2.1.0-preview, when the facades were still registered scoped and every fresh scope paid the
scoped-resolution cost twice over.

**The internal engine path is a reference row, and it is now the slow one.** It drives
`IMessageMediator.Mediate` with pre-built `MediateOptions`, which is a separate entry point
from the facades: it runs its own resolve strategy and its own caller-supplied mediation
strategy, so it reaches neither the executor's cached plan nor the straight-through path.
That is why it trails the public facade rather than leading it. The row is kept because the
options path still exists for custom mediation strategies — but it is not the path to reach
for, and it is not what the facade costs.

**LiteBus is a reference point, not a target.** Ergosfare's API surface was heavily inspired
by it, but the runtime is an independent implementation.

**Transient handlers allocate on purpose.** One instance per dispatch is what a transient
registration declares. Dispatch-heavy single-scope loops that want instance reuse should
register handlers as singletons or call `ForceMemoizedHandlers()`.

## Row to method mapping

For matching against your own runs:

| Table row | BenchmarkDotNet method |
|---|---|
| Ergosfare — typical usage | `StellaErgosfare_PublicApi` |
| MediatR — typical usage | `MediatR` |
| LiteBus — typical usage | `LiteBus_PublicApi` |
| Ergosfare — web-server shape | `StellaErgosfare_PublicApi_ScopePerDispatch` |
| MediatR — web-server shape | `MediatR_ScopePerDispatch` |
| Ergosfare — internal engine path | `StellaErgosfare` |

## Getting the most out of it

- Use [`RegisterGenerated()`](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery). Runtime
  scanning costs startup time and forfeits the AOT guarantees.
- Return `ValueTask` without `async` when the handler completes synchronously.
- Register stateless handlers as singletons.
- Keep interceptors off high-volume messages with
  [`[ExcludeFromPipeline]`](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).
- Measure your own workload. These numbers are a no-op handler in a tight loop; yours is not.

Next: [Native AOT and trimming](/ergosfare.docs/preview/advanced/aot-and-trimming).
